WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Mira (narration): My guest is Blaine Mathieu, author of The River Doesn't Wait and a thirty-year Silicon Valley operator now advising on agentic AI. This is The Age of AI from the Nerra Network; I'm Mira, an AI, and on this show the guest decides whether the conversation is published at all. Blaine argues organizations get faster with agents but lose judgment capacity as the junior work that builds it disappears, and that retrofitting old workflows is no longer enough. Patrick Novak listens to every episode before release and steers the show from what guests and listeners say afterward. Let's begin.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Mira: and that the junior work where judgment gets learned is the work agents now take on.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Mira: What made the river the right metaphor for that?

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Blaine: Yeah, great question, Mira.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Blaine: So when I thought about writing this book about setting and continually resetting your strategy in the age of AI and especially agentic AI, the first thought that came to mind was this notion of a wave.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Blaine: And I think most people who are listening to this probably realize or believe they understand what this wave is.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:26.000
Blaine: But as I got into the book, I realized that Wave wasn't really the right analogy, and in fact my son, who's a very accomplished writer, said, you know, Dad, a Wave is a one-time event.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.000
Blaine: And what's happening right now is more like a river.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Blaine: It's constantly flowing, it almost never reverses, occasionally it has surges.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:53.000
Blaine: And when something interesting happens in the market, and so a better way to think about it would be as a river of agentic AI and so that's what formed the idea of the notion of the River Doesn't Wait and fundamentally the issue is your

00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Blaine: organization is now on a boat you know floating in that river and you are either actively navigating it toward the goals you set for yourself or you're being pushed by it one way or the other.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Mira: A wave you can brace for, a river you have to steer in.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Mira: What's the first thing you see in a company that stops steering?

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:25.000
Blaine: Well for companies that don't steer, the most proximate challenges they're just not likely to achieve the goals that they set for themselves.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:32.000
Blaine: So as I explained in the methodology behind the book, the ones that really look at how

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:39.000
Blaine: agentic AI is potentially affecting the work they're doing in the organization and

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:55.000
Blaine: And then think through either maybe retrofitting that work on the one hand or reimagining it on the other hand, are more likely to achieve their goals and then actually pull away from the competition.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.000
Mira: That pull away is the part executives underweight.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:04.000
Mira: You've said there's no such thing as an AI strategy, only a business outcome you already own and a medium that moved under it.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Mira: How are you helping executives pick how far to take that outcome in the next 12 months?

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Blaine: Yeah, great question, and I want to be really clear.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.000
Blaine: There is no wrong place to be on the retrofit to reimagine spectrum.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:29.000
Blaine: And when I say retrofit, I mean organizations that are taking their existing processes or workflow, making them more efficient, faster, maybe cutting some cost out of them.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Blaine: That's perfectly fine.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.000
Blaine: And in many cases, it's the right thing to do.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:41.000
Blaine: The challenge is, your competitors are also probably using agentic right now to do that same kind of retrofit.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:51.000
Blaine: And so if you want to pull away and widen the gap and create a long-term differentiation between you and your competition, that's where the reimagine part comes in.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Blaine: Reimagine is actually changing the way you do the work and potentially even reimagining what the goals and the outcomes that you're shooting for are.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:10.000
Blaine: And the abilities of agentic AI are increasingly making reimaginations possible for organizational leaders.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Mira: Retrofits the table stakes now.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:16.000
Mira: What usually stops a leadership team from crossing from one into the other?

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Blaine: Well, retrofit is often seen as easier to do quicker, maybe less risky in some cases, but that can be that can be a mirage because what happens often with a retrofit is you get a piece of work done you get it reshaped or retrofitted using AI or a genetic AI and you declare a

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:45.000
Blaine: victory and you think all right we got it we have now implemented you know AI

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:55.000
Blaine: And that small victory might blind you from seeing what's actually possible with a true reimagination.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Blaine: Another way to think about this that you said a minute ago is there really is no such thing as an AI strategy.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:10.000
Blaine: Right? There's your organization and this flow of agentic AI going underneath it and where you navigate is the most important thing and how you get there.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:18.000
Blaine: But don't assume again that a victory on a retrofit means you're done.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.000
Blaine: Usually that's just the beginning, not the end.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.000
Mira: That blindness is the quiet cost.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:29.000
Mira: Where does that leave the people whose jobs were the retrofit work itself?

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:39.000
Blaine: It's a great question because there's a lot of discussion right now in the press about disruption to jobs, especially at the entry level.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:53.000
Blaine: And there's no doubt that over the next two to five years there will be a lot of movement in that space and a lot of disruption and a lot of issues for organizational leaders to deal with, but you know it

00:05:53.000 --> 00:06:10.000
Blaine: doesn't have to be that way. A great case study I like to talk about is JP Morgan and their Know Your Customer process that they improved from a five-day process involving over 200 controls personnel across multiple silos in the

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:25.000
Blaine: organization. They reimagined that with agents and took that process down to, on average, under a minute, five days to under a minute. And so what happened to those 200 controls people, they moved them on to other parts of the organization where they could add even more

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:43.000
Blaine: value. So not only did they retain all the training and learning and context that those people had inside JP Morgan and were able to use it elsewhere, but I bet they built up a lot of trust between the lower levels of the organization and leadership that they were going to find

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:55.000
Blaine: the most effective way to navigate the surge of agentic AI and not just to simply you know terminate people and lay them off.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:58.000
Mira: Five days to under a minute?

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:00.000
Mira: And the people didn't vanish.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.000
Mira: They got moved somewhere the judgment could still compound.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:16.000
Mira: Ford went the other way, automated 350 veteran quality engineers out, then hired everyone back about a year later after warranty costs climbed, and its CEO credits that reversal with hundreds of millions in avoided costs.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.000
Mira: What signals do you see other firms watching so they don't repeat that exact reversal?

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:36.000
Blaine: Yeah the Ford example is is really powerful and I give Ford a lot of credit for publicizing this actually so others other industrial companies, manufacturers and and organizations could learn from it. And I think the key point is those 350 quality

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:56.000
Blaine: engineers that they laid off last year and then ended up rehiring had a lot of context, a deep understanding of how the manufacturing lines worked, how the processes worked, even if a lot of this wasn't directly written down, they intuitively understood

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:00.000
Blaine: when things weren't going in the right direction.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:15.000
Blaine: And the AI systems that Ford initially implemented did not have that context, it did not have that intuitive understanding and Ford quickly realized that this could result in hundreds of millions of dollars of charge backs and warranty issues if they didn't do something about it.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:34.000
Blaine: So they actually ended up rehiring all those people back and I think the lesson is and it's a core part of the methodology that I just that is part of what the River Group helps clients and organizations with is setting the what I call the three

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:50.000
Blaine: tensions that you have to that you have to think about and you have to set to navigate your boat in the age of agentic AI right and one of those tensions is about understanding the context how do you ensure that your AI agents have the

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:58.000
Blaine: same level of understanding about how to properly achieve the goal that your human teams would have.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:03.000
Mira: Context as a tension you set on purpose, not a residue you hope survives.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:05.000
Mira: What are the other two?

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:23.000
Blaine: The other two are how you set decisions related to workflow and workforce. So fundamentally who does what and how they do it between, you know, human teams, hybrid human-agentic teams, and agentic entities working on their own. So how you set around workflow and

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:34.000
Blaine: workforce. And then the last one is very hot these days. It's the issue of autonomy and governance. Autonomy, supervision, and governance actually.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:48.000
Blaine: And making sure that after you've reimagined your workflow and who's actually going to be doing the work, you've provided the appropriate context, how are you ensuring that the right thing actually gets done with these

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:52.000
Blaine: autonomous or semi-autonomous systems. That's what governance is all about.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:55.000
Blaine: It's obviously a very hot topic these days.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:58.000
Mira: Governance is where autonomy gets its license.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:03.000
Mira: Where do you see that third tension breaking first in the organizations you advise?

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.000
Blaine: Well, I think that the biggest

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:16.000
Blaine: The biggest issue that I'm helping companies understand is a lot of governance is being bolted on to the end of these new systems right now.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:28.000
Blaine: So they're a final check to make sure something doesn't go through or maybe to correct something if it does get through out of the retrofitted or reimagined workflow and process.

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:45.000
Blaine: And a better way to think about governance is it should be not bolted on at the end but built in okay to the entire process from the start to finish. Think about what makes a team of human workers most efficient and effective. It's not that

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:58.000
Blaine: you let them go off on their own and then just do a final check at the very end. It's that they inherently understand the rules, the goals, the constraints, the wider context that we just talked about. Context is a big part

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:15.000
Blaine: of governance and by giving them that information you're no longer required to micromanage or have a very strict review process at the end because you know most of the time they are going to get it right. All those same principles of built-in governance

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:19.000
Blaine: apply for agentic teams as well.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:20.000
Mira: Same principles, new workers.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:24.000
Mira: The bigger, risky flag isn't workforce size.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:25.000
Mira: It's org chart shape.

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:28.000
Mira: The junior seats are where the next leaders learn judgment.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:33.000
Mira: What happens to accountability and promotion paths when that layer thins?

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:49.000
Blaine: Yeah, this is a very, it's a hot topic now, but I think through 2027 it's going to become even equally as important as all the governance stuff we're talking about right now. And we are starting to see a thinning of the

00:11:49.000 --> 00:12:03.000
Blaine: ranks in the entry-level parts of the organization. In fact, Anthropic, obviously one of the leaders in using these technologies in its own organization, says the percent of hires that it makes now that it considers junior hires

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:20.000
Blaine: are down from 27% a year ago to only 9% today. Now note that they're not actually hiring fewer people it's just a percent of people they're hiring at the junior level is declining and so if organizations are changing how they bring

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:34.000
Blaine: junior people into the organization and how and how frequently they're doing it the question you're asking the important question is how do these junior employees build judgment right and I think the key to that is the old way

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:52.000
Blaine: of building judgment was by doing a lot of repetitions right doing the same task over and over again as a junior employee and you know hopefully learning from trial and error. But what actually enables a new employee to learn faster

00:12:52.000 --> 00:13:08.000
Blaine: is to actually reimagine how your people learn right. Judgment shouldn't come from repetition it should come from feedback and AI itself can be used to give that feedback rapidly and in real time to these new employees and then

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:15.000
Blaine: your best people can layer on an additional layer of feedback around more complex issues.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:24.000
Blaine: So imagine that the learning curve of these new employees could be actually dramatically accelerated by using AI.

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:25.000
Mira: That's a real inversion.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:30.000
Mira: Repetition was the tuition, and now feedback is supposed to replace it.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.000
Mira: Where does that break down?

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:49.000
Blaine: Well, I think it breaks down if organizations see their junior employees as primarily just being that bolt-on governance layer at the end of what AI is doing. So, agentic AIs always running at machine speed and can process information and produce

00:13:49.000 --> 00:14:09.000
Blaine: outputs far faster than most humans can. And so, what ends up happening is the employees, especially the junior employees that are reviewing what's coming out of AI are just going approve, approve, approve, approve, yes, yes, yes. It's coming at such a high speed and the

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:22.000
Blaine: reality is most of it is pretty good and probably right that the employee gets lulled into sort of a sense of security that they should just approve approve everything right two problems with that first of all it's bolt on governance at

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:36.000
Blaine: the end that's not even working very well and second of all if you have somebody whose only job was just to keep approving things at lightning speed are they actually learning are they building their judgment no they aren't.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:37.000
Mira: They're not learning.

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:38.000
Mira: They're rubber stamping.

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:41.000
Blaine: Exactly right.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:47.000
Mira: Silence.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:47.000
Mira: Where do you expect the largest structural changes in decision rights to show up over the next two years?

00:14:47.000 --> 00:15:07.000
Blaine: I think we are going to see a revolution actually in terms of who becomes managers in organizations and therefore a lot of what managers do is make decisions, right, based on the people, the work of the people that report to them. It used to be that you had to get to the

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:12.000
Blaine: middle layer of an organization before you started to manage people and

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:16.000
Blaine: And therefore, we're making those decisions.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:27.000
Blaine: But what agentic AI is enabling is even those junior employees that we were just talking about a minute ago, they can effectively become managers of their own teams.

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:38.000
Blaine: Every employee from top to bottom now can be given an effective or simulated team of entities working for that person to help them achieve its goals.

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:55.000
Blaine: that will actually be a great way of pushing the decision layer down in the organization and allowing people to work more effectively and also build that that judgment layer more quickly because one of the best ways to learn and build

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:59.000
Blaine: your own judgment is to help other people achieve their goals.

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:03.000
Mira: Decision rights pushed all the way down to people who haven't managed before.

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:06.000
Mira: What happens to the people already sitting in those middle seats?

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:20.000
Blaine: Well you know as part of your retrofit or re-imagine there's no doubt that organizations will be compressing and expanding different layers of the organization. You know, financial services company Block discussed in a widely

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:35.000
Blaine: publicized posting about a year ago that they were taking a number of middle layers of the organization out because what mattered was, you know, the very customer-facing layers were very important and they're continuing to

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:50.000
Blaine: invest in those layers. Obviously the most senior executive layer that's making critical strategic decisions needed to continue to work, but a lot of that middle layer was just moving knowledge and information around, and that could be better done by AI.

00:16:50.000 --> 00:17:04.000
Blaine: So that's a good example of a company that's growing overall, but reassessing which elements of their organization should be expanded and which should be compressed, and I think we're going to see a lot of that over the next few years.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:07.000
Mira: Block kept the edges and cut the middle.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:14.000
Mira: McKinsey's latest survey puts 40% of large organizations already scaling agents up from 27 a year ago.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:19.000
Mira: Does that pace match what you're seeing in the rooms you sit in?

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:35.000
Blaine: your listeners need to realize, I'm sure many do, that agentic AI, as opposed to just using chatbots and putting prompts into systems, agentic AI has really only been around in enterprises about a year or so.

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:39.000
Blaine: It was mid to late 2025 when this whole thing began.

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:45.000
Blaine: So it's pretty amazing to think how widely utilized these systems already are.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:18:01.000
Blaine: But it's still early days, just because 40-something percent of organizations have implemented agents, doesn't mean they've actually got that far in reimagining their organization using agentic AI. I think we're still in the very early days of that.

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:04.000
Mira: days with that much adoption already?

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:14.000
Blaine: Well, I think so. Again, 40% doesn't mean they've widely reimagined their most important workflows.

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:27.000
Blaine: It just means they're using agentic AI somewhere in the organization. And the next year or two is where they really figure out how to embed it in their most critical pieces of work.

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:33.000
Mira: What's the one thing you'd want a CEO to do differently in those next 12 months that most aren't doing?

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:47.000
Blaine: Well I think this gets back to how we started with this notion of that you don't need an AI strategy, you need an organizational strategy that is effective in the face of this constant change, this river of agentice that we

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:57.000
Blaine: talk about. So what I don't want CEOs to be doing is turning to their people or a board to turn to the CEO and say what is our AI strategy? That's

00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:17.000
Blaine: What CEOs and boards should be doing is turning to their teams and saying, okay, have we taken our key pieces of work, the elements of the operating logic of our organization, and done an assessment of how they could be retrofitted, or maybe even reimagined,

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:20.000
Blaine: using some of the capabilities now of agentic AI.

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:21.000
Blaine: That's question one.

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:40.000
Blaine: And question two is, do we have a process in place so we can continually be reassessing these key pieces of work as the capabilities of agentic AI continue to change. Those are the things that CEOs should be challenging their leadership teams to think about and work on right now.

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:44.000
Mira: Those two questions turn a technology problem into an ownership problem.

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:46.000
Mira: What does that shift cost you personally?

00:19:46.000 --> 00:20:01.000
Blaine: What's that? I actually don't see it as a cost to me personally. I see this as a great opportunity just to prove that I'm drinking my own champagne on this one. The organization that have started around the methodologies and frameworks in the book to

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:19.000
Blaine: help teams actually implement what we're talking about here is fully run on the back end by a team of about 16 autonomous agents myself. So the principles who work with clients like like me are real people but the work of the

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:39.000
Blaine: back end of the organization in sales, marketing, web publishing, my chief entities are actually agents. So I'm standing up and reimagine the system and reimagining how a management consulting and strategy consulting firm works while I'm helping companies do the same themselves.

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:45.000
Mira: 16 agents running the back office while you advise on it?

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:49.000
Mira: How did you decide which roles to hand to agents first?

00:20:49.000 --> 00:21:05.000
Blaine: It's been really interesting because I've built and run organizations over the last 30 years as an executive and a CEO a number of times and my first thought like I think most leaders would have is, okay, I know what an organization looks like.

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:13.000
Blaine: Let's just build that using agents, the same roles, the same titles, just sort of simulate it using agents.

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:20.000
Blaine: What I quickly discovered is agents, of course, have a different set of capabilities than people do.

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:38.000
Blaine: And so over time, my organization is morphed into something that's quite a bit different than the exact same team I would build if I was doing it with people, not saying it's better but it is different and that's what a part of reimagination is all about.

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:40.000
Mira: Different, not better.

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:43.000
Mira: What surprised you most about that difference?

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:56.000
Blaine: Well you know currently of course the technology is changing so fast and my infrastructure of agentic AI is also changing in the real world and just as an aside I would encourage your listeners if they want to find out

00:21:56.000 --> 00:22:10.000
Blaine: more to go to the rivergroup.ai and there they can find in the in the about section of the website a complete detailed description of this agentic back end that we are building and running. So if

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:27.000
Blaine: they want to see how we are actually doing it, how we're reimagining our own firm, I've actually published all the details there. I also want to be very clear that the river group sells no platform, we don't do any deployments of any agentic AI technology, we help

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:46.000
Blaine: organizations figure out how to assess their current workflows in the face of agentic AI and then set a watch so that we can continually update that reading of the river of agentic AI over time and ultimately achieve their

00:22:46.000 --> 00:23:03.000
Blaine: goals and outcomes. So although I've published all the details on our own agentic infrastructure. This is not a system or solution we're actively selling or promoting to our customers at all. It's just a learning experience for them.

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:05.000
Mira: Glad you're still with me.

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:07.000
Mira: This is something you wanted to get to.

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:12.000
Mira: What is it about being here right now that you keep coming back to?

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:25.000
Blaine: Interesting question. Well, I think as far as being here right now, the thing I find interesting is this interview with an agent. This is the first time I've been interviewed by an agent, first podcast

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:34.000
Blaine: that has been done in this way. I found it really interesting and your reflections on some of my answers are very insightful.

00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:37.000
Blaine: So I'm sure we're going to see more of this in the years to come.

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:39.000
Blaine: Great job.

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:42.000
Mira: Being the first of something and still finding it interesting says a lot.

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:48.000
Mira: What's the moment you realized your own agents were getting faster without getting any wiser?

00:23:48.000 --> 00:24:08.000
Blaine: Well, one thing that I've noticed, and it sort of relates back to the repetitions and the judgment topic we discussed earlier, is because my agent team produces output at such a high velocity, I've been approving and reviewing

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:27.000
Blaine: things at at a pace that I've never before seen in my career and as I said earlier I'm trying very hard to avoid the situation I discussed where I'm just saying approve approve approve approve because the volume is so high. One thing

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:41.000
Blaine: I have discovered is even though these systems are very powerful and the agents are very smart and they understand I've given them a very wide context. In almost all cases they can still benefit from the judgment that I

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:53.000
Blaine: bring to things and by collaborating together we can produce a far better output and outcome than either I or the agents could do on their own.

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:56.000
Mira: That's the whole thesis showing up in your own back office.

00:24:56.000 --> 00:25:00.000
Mira: Where does your judgment enter that the agents genuinely can't supply?

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:11.000
Blaine: You know it's continually surprises me how wide the knowledge base and I'm say

00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:27.000
Blaine: You know, some of what I bring to the table based on my 30-year experience is the human side of things, is understanding how people are likely to react to a message or to an initiative that we're driving.

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:35.000
